      "summary": "The idea proposes black-box detection of data-sensitive DBMS performance anomalies via a workload generator that creates random schemas, incremental data, and distribution-sensitive queries. It compares against existing baselines on six DBMSs using Docker isolation. The angle is relevant, but the artifact provides only a high-level problem description and evaluation constraints rather than a concrete technical approach. Novelty is incremental over data-generation and performance-bug-definition prior work, clarity is low, and feasibility is constrained by the difficult engineering task of efficiently triggering rare performance cliffs without manual guidance.",
      "strengths": [
        "Targets a practically important DBMS performance problem.",
        "Black-box Docker setup and six-DBMS comparison provide a clear empirical context.",
        "Builds on established baselines and identifies relevant metrics such as tail latency and execution time."
      ],
